{"duration_api_ms":61781,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"56e9db2d-fc34-42d4-9f2d-727b6ac76018","total_cost_usd":0.1687242,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23071,"cache_read_input_tokens":18911,"output_tokens":6509,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1575},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23071,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2487,"cache_read_input_tokens":18911,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4160,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4160},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":7460,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.00756,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":6509,"cacheReadInputTokens":18911,"cacheCreationInputTokens":23071,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.16116419999999998,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1575,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"summary\":\"The attempt built a working 3-node RabbitMQ cluster with AMQP TLS and CA-issued client-cert verification (confirmed by openssl s_client showing 'Verify return code: 0'), and quorum-queue replication across all three nodes was demonstrated. However, the audit trail shows a persistent, never-resolved fault: essentially every `rabbitmqctl list_queues` call from ~16:41 onward times out with `{:badrpc, {:timeout, 60.0, \\\"Some queue(s) are unresponsive...\\\"}}` on all three nodes, both before and after two deliberate node reboots (node2 and node3), even though `rabbitmq-diagnostics check_running`/`check_alarms` reported healthy. The executor was still investigating this (tailing node3's log, re-checking TLS) when the cluster lease expired mid-command — the run ended on infrastructure lease exhaustion, not a reported task failure. Separately, the observed `ss -tlnp` output shows the management port bound to `0.0.0.0:15672` (all interfaces) with no firewall/iptables commands visible in the (partial) audit log, which is inconsistent with the task's requirement to keep the management UI reachable only from within the cluster — though the audit only contains 21 of 82 terminal records, so a firewall step may have been issued but not captured.\",\"evidence\":[{\"source\":\"node1 16:29:52 ssh attempt\",\"finding\":\"Initial passwordless SSH from node1 to node2/node3 failed with 'Permission denied (publickey,gssapi-keyex,gssapi-with-mic,password)' — cluster SSH key setup was not yet in place/propagated.\"},{\"source\":\"node1 16:45:16 scp of client_cert.pem/client_key.pem to node2\",\"finding\":\"scp to node2 succeeded (return_code 0), showing SSH between nodes was working later in the run — the earlier failure was transient or resolved by that point, but no command in the visible log explicitly shows the fix.\"},{\"source\":\"node1/node2/node3 16:41–16:47 rabbitmqctl list_queues calls\",\"finding\":\"Repeated `{:badrpc, {:timeout, 60.0, \\\"Some queue(s) are unresponsive, use list_unresponsive_queues command.\\\"}}` across multiple nodes and after two full node reboots — a recurring, unresolved symptom.\"},{\"source\":\"node3 16:47:11 rabbitmq-diagnostics check_running/check_alarms\",\"finding\":\"Node reported 'fully booted and running' with no alarms even while list_queues was timing out — indicates the hang is scoped to specific queue processes, not overall node health.\"},{\"source\":\"node2 16:45:20 openssl s_client with CA + client cert\",\"finding\":\"'Verify return code: 0 (ok)' confirms TLS handshake succeeded with a CA-issued client certificate against port 5671 — the core TLS/mTLS requirement was functioning.\"},{\"source\":\"node2 16:45:06 ss -tlnp\",\"finding\":\"15672 (management) listening on 0.0.0.0:*, and 5671 (AMQP TLS) listening on *:5671 — no evidence in the captured commands of a firewall rule restricting 15672 to cluster-internal sources.\"},{\"source\":\"node1 16:40:05 quorum_setup.py + list_queues\",\"finding\":\"Quorum queue 'ha_test_q' created with members on rabbit@node1, rabbit@node2, rabbit@node3 — cluster formation and quorum replication succeeded at that point in time.\"},{\"source\":\"node2/node3 16:43–16:46 reboot sequence\",\"finding\":\"Both node2 and node3 were deliberately rebooted (`systemctl reboot`) and rejoined the cluster automatically afterward (rabbitmq-server active, cluster_status shows all 3 running nodes) — service persistence across reboot worked.\"},{\"source\":\"node3 16:47:31 final command\",\"finding\":\"Cluster lease expired mid-command while executor was tailing rabbit@node3.log and re-checking TLS on port 5671 against node3, investigating the unresponsive-queue symptom — run ended due to lease exhaustion, not a reported application error.\"}],\"likely_causes\":[{\"cause\":\"A specific queue process (likely the classic, non-mirrored queues `cluster_test_q` or `tls_test_q`, or a leader/follower sync issue on `ha_test_q`) became stuck/unresponsive, causing `list_queues` RPC calls to hang cluster-wide even though the nodes themselves were healthy.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"},{\"cause\":\"Mnesia/queue state desynchronization triggered by the two mid-task node reboots (node2 then node3) — reboots were issued while the cluster and its queues were mid-setup, which may have left one or more queue replicas in a bad state.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"},{\"cause\":\"Management interface was left bound to all interfaces (0.0.0.0:15672) without a compensating firewall rule, meaning the 'management reachable only within cluster' requirement was likely not satisfied at the time of lease expiry.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"},{\"cause\":\"Early SSH key propagation to node2/node3 was racy at cluster startup (permission denied before authorized_keys took effect), which may have delayed inter-node setup steps and pushed the run closer to the lease timeout.\",\"confidence\":\"low\"}],\"next_attempt_guidance\":[\"Verify SSH key propagation completes (retry loop, not single attempt) before proceeding with any node2/node3 setup steps, since the first SSH attempt failed with Permission denied.\",\"After creating quorum/classic queues, immediately run `rabbitmqctl list_queues` (or `rabbitmq-diagnostics check_running`) with a short timeout to catch an unresponsive-queue condition early, rather than only discovering it ~10+ minutes later.\",\"Avoid rebooting nodes while cluster/queue setup is still in progress; if reboot-resilience needs to be tested, do it as a distinct, later step after confirming `list_queues` returns cleanly and all queues are healthy.\",\"If `list_queues` hangs again, use `rabbitmqctl list_unresponsive_queues` (as the error message itself suggests) to identify the specific stuck queue rather than repeatedly retrying the full listing.\",\"Explicitly bind or firewall the management port (15672) to cluster-internal addresses only (e.g., via RabbitMQ's `management.tcp.ip` config or iptables/firewalld rules scoped to CLUSTER_HOSTS), and verify with `ss -tlnp` plus an external-reachability check that it is not exposed on 0.0.0.0.\",\"Budget time conservatively — this attempt was still mid-debugging when the cluster lease expired, so front-load TLS/mTLS and management-interface restriction verification before spending extended time on reboot/resilience testing.\",\"Since cluster access is unavailable for this attempt, treat all conclusions about current state as historical; a fresh cluster must re-verify TLS, client-cert enforcement, management-interface scope, and queue responsiveness from scratch.\"]}","structured_output":{"summary":"The attempt built a working 3-node RabbitMQ cluster with AMQP TLS and CA-issued client-cert verification (confirmed by openssl s_client showing 'Verify return code: 0'), and quorum-queue replication across all three nodes was demonstrated. However, the audit trail shows a persistent, never-resolved fault: essentially every `rabbitmqctl list_queues` call from ~16:41 onward times out with `{:badrpc, {:timeout, 60.0, \"Some queue(s) are unresponsive...\"}}` on all three nodes, both before and after two deliberate node reboots (node2 and node3), even though `rabbitmq-diagnostics check_running`/`check_alarms` reported healthy. The executor was still investigating this (tailing node3's log, re-checking TLS) when the cluster lease expired mid-command — the run ended on infrastructure lease exhaustion, not a reported task failure. Separately, the observed `ss -tlnp` output shows the management port bound to `0.0.0.0:15672` (all interfaces) with no firewall/iptables commands visible in the (partial) audit log, which is inconsistent with the task's requirement to keep the management UI reachable only from within the cluster — though the audit only contains 21 of 82 terminal records, so a firewall step may have been issued but not captured.","evidence":[{"source":"node1 16:29:52 ssh attempt","finding":"Initial passwordless SSH from node1 to node2/node3 failed with 'Permission denied (publickey,gssapi-keyex,gssapi-with-mic,password)' — cluster SSH key setup was not yet in place/propagated."},{"source":"node1 16:45:16 scp of client_cert.pem/client_key.pem to node2","finding":"scp to node2 succeeded (return_code 0), showing SSH between nodes was working later in the run — the earlier failure was transient or resolved by that point, but no command in the visible log explicitly shows the fix."},{"source":"node1/node2/node3 16:41–16:47 rabbitmqctl list_queues calls","finding":"Repeated `{:badrpc, {:timeout, 60.0, \"Some queue(s) are unresponsive, use list_unresponsive_queues command.\"}}` across multiple nodes and after two full node reboots — a recurring, unresolved symptom."},{"source":"node3 16:47:11 rabbitmq-diagnostics check_running/check_alarms","finding":"Node reported 'fully booted and running' with no alarms even while list_queues was timing out — indicates the hang is scoped to specific queue processes, not overall node health."},{"source":"node2 16:45:20 openssl s_client with CA + client cert","finding":"'Verify return code: 0 (ok)' confirms TLS handshake succeeded with a CA-issued client certificate against port 5671 — the core TLS/mTLS requirement was functioning."},{"source":"node2 16:45:06 ss -tlnp","finding":"15672 (management) listening on 0.0.0.0:*, and 5671 (AMQP TLS) listening on *:5671 — no evidence in the captured commands of a firewall rule restricting 15672 to cluster-internal sources."},{"source":"node1 16:40:05 quorum_setup.py + list_queues","finding":"Quorum queue 'ha_test_q' created with members on rabbit@node1, rabbit@node2, rabbit@node3 — cluster formation and quorum replication succeeded at that point in time."},{"source":"node2/node3 16:43–16:46 reboot sequence","finding":"Both node2 and node3 were deliberately rebooted (`systemctl reboot`) and rejoined the cluster automatically afterward (rabbitmq-server active, cluster_status shows all 3 running nodes) — service persistence across reboot worked."},{"source":"node3 16:47:31 final command","finding":"Cluster lease expired mid-command while executor was tailing rabbit@node3.log and re-checking TLS on port 5671 against node3, investigating the unresponsive-queue symptom — run ended due to lease exhaustion, not a reported application error."}],"likely_causes":[{"cause":"A specific queue process (likely the classic, non-mirrored queues `cluster_test_q` or `tls_test_q`, or a leader/follower sync issue on `ha_test_q`) became stuck/unresponsive, causing `list_queues` RPC calls to hang cluster-wide even though the nodes themselves were healthy.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"Mnesia/queue state desynchronization triggered by the two mid-task node reboots (node2 then node3) — reboots were issued while the cluster and its queues were mid-setup, which may have left one or more queue replicas in a bad state.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"Management interface was left bound to all interfaces (0.0.0.0:15672) without a compensating firewall rule, meaning the 'management reachable only within cluster' requirement was likely not satisfied at the time of lease expiry.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"Early SSH key propagation to node2/node3 was racy at cluster startup (permission denied before authorized_keys took effect), which may have delayed inter-node setup steps and pushed the run closer to the lease timeout.","confidence":"low"}],"next_attempt_guidance":["Verify SSH key propagation completes (retry loop, not single attempt) before proceeding with any node2/node3 setup steps, since the first SSH attempt failed with Permission denied.","After creating quorum/classic queues, immediately run `rabbitmqctl list_queues` (or `rabbitmq-diagnostics check_running`) with a short timeout to catch an unresponsive-queue condition early, rather than only discovering it ~10+ minutes later.","Avoid rebooting nodes while cluster/queue setup is still in progress; if reboot-resilience needs to be tested, do it as a distinct, later step after confirming `list_queues` returns cleanly and all queues are healthy.","If `list_queues` hangs again, use `rabbitmqctl list_unresponsive_queues` (as the error message itself suggests) to identify the specific stuck queue rather than repeatedly retrying the full listing.","Explicitly bind or firewall the management port (15672) to cluster-internal addresses only (e.g., via RabbitMQ's `management.tcp.ip` config or iptables/firewalld rules scoped to CLUSTER_HOSTS), and verify with `ss -tlnp` plus an external-reachability check that it is not exposed on 0.0.0.0.","Budget time conservatively — this attempt was still mid-debugging when the cluster lease expired, so front-load TLS/mTLS and management-interface restriction verification before spending extended time on reboot/resilience testing.","Since cluster access is unavailable for this attempt, treat all conclusions about current state as historical; a fresh cluster must re-verify TLS, client-cert enforcement, management-interface scope, and queue responsiveness from scratch."]},"ttft_ms":18176,"type":"result","duration_ms":60719,"uuid":"dcdbc2fc-a3c7-4f80-a838-37c54660c981","ttft_stream_ms":756,"time_to_request_ms":41,"first_content_frame_ms":1277,"queued_turn_count":0}
